// Regression test: `assert()` with zero arguments must be a clean compile
// error, not a compiler crash.
//
// Root cause: `assert` is registered in Builtins.cpp with BuiltinInfo::Arity
// == -1 ("variadic/unknown") because it genuinely accepts either 1 or 2 args
// (condition, optional message) -- not a single fixed count. That disabled
// TypeChecker::visitFuncCall's generic single-arity check entirely, so a
// 0-arg (or 3+-arg) call was never rejected here. CodegenIntrinsic's
// "assert" handler unconditionally reads Args[0] with no bounds check,
// trusting arity was already validated -- so `assert();` reached an
// out-of-bounds std::vector access and crashed the compiler (SIGABRT), and
// worse, did so via main.cpp's pre-pass codegen (sub-pass 3b), which runs
// unconditionally even when the pre-pass's own TypeChecker instance already
// flagged the same call as invalid (those errors are deliberately discarded
// there -- "informational only... main-pass TC will re-check"). See
// src/TypeChecker.cpp's visitFuncCall doc comment on the assert/fail
// branches, and src/Builtins.cpp's defensive Args.size() checks, for the
// full fix (an explicit 1-or-2 arity check plus bounds-checked codegen as
// defense in depth).

fn main(): int {
    assert();
    return 0;
}
